 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Maangchi lists <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tsp\" data-a=\"3\" data-s=\"0\">3 tsp</span> sugar, then uses <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tsp\" data-a=\"1\" data-s=\"0\">1 tsp</span> for the pear’s sugar water and just “sugar” in the sauce.</li><li>It’s not clear whether the <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tsp\" data-a=\"3\" data-s=\"0\">3 tsp</span> includes the sugar water.</li><li>Read here as <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tsp\" data-a=\"2\" data-s=\"0\">2 tsp</span> in the sauce; use 3 if you like it sweeter.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "The sugar (unclear on the page)"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>The page says “½ teaspoon ginger” without saying fresh or ground.</li><li>Fresh, minced is assumed here — it all goes through the food processor anyway.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "The ginger"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>If you can’t get naengmyeon noodles: <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"g\" data-a=\"100\" data-s=\"0\">100 g</span> dried soba per person, cooked and rinsed as in <a href=\"zaru-soba.html\">zaru soba</a>.</li><li>Maangchi notes that soba aren’t nearly as chewy or elastic as naengmyeon.</li><li>Soba also has no broth packet.</li><li><b>Our substitute (untested):</b> zaru soba’s dipping-sauce concentrate diluted about 1 : 5 with iced water and chilled, a ladle per bowl — or skip the broth for a drier bibim-style bowl.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "With Japanese soba instead"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Make as much or as little sauce as you like.</li><li>Leftovers keep in the fridge for a few weeks.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Extra sauce"
  }
 ],
